  "worked_solution": "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}113 & ≡11\\cdot 10+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡11\\cdot 0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 119 & ≡11\\cdot 10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡11\\cdot 0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}113\\cdot 119 & ≡3\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡27\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 10+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n113\\cdot 119\\text{ mod }10=7.\n$$",
